{res}Table 3 | Supermarket access and individual energy and macronutrient intake
{txt}{hline 110}
Study: Supermarkets benefit women by reducing time spent on unpaid housework without lowering nutritional quality.
Data: China Health and Nutrition Survey, 2004, 2006, 2009 and 2011; unweighted panel analysis.
Treatment: supermarket access within 5 km. Always-treated communities are excluded.
Controls: age, age squared, dietary knowledge, household size, ownership of a car, motorcycle,
refrigerator, microwave and pressure cooker, traditional market development, and local economic activity.
Fixed effects: individual and province by survey period.
Standard errors: clustered by community and sex.
All numerical results below are recomputed from the supplied analysis data in Stata 18 MP.
N reports observations used by the estimator; model input counts are shown separately.
Significance: *** p<0.01; ** p<0.05; * p<0.10. Confidence level: 95%.
Estimator: CS-DID regression adjustment, never-treated controls; overall ATET.
Sample: adult women and men. Outcomes are daily per-capita intakes.
Panels: A energy (kcal/day); B protein, C fat, D carbohydrates (grams/day).
Columns: (1) total; (2) unprocessed; (3) moderately processed; (4) highly processed foods.

